WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.499
Ashoka's inscriptions stood for two

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:03.798
thousand years.

3
00:00:03.798 --> 00:00:06.774
No one could read them,

4
00:00:06.774 --> 00:00:08.603
until 1837.

5
00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.018
In 1837,

6
00:00:10.018 --> 00:00:13.595
James Prinsep deciphered the Brahmi

7
00:00:13.595 --> 00:00:15.338
script. Suddenly,

8
00:00:15.338 --> 00:00:17.614
Ashoka's edicts could speak.

9
00:00:17.945 --> 00:00:21.082
Archaeological sources include inscriptions and

10
00:00:21.082 --> 00:00:22.803
coins, monuments,

11
00:00:22.803 --> 00:00:24.730
and excavated sites.

12
00:00:25.063 --> 00:00:27.850
Literary sources include Indian texts,

13
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.632
from the Vedas to Sangam

14
00:00:29.632 --> 00:00:32.155
poetry. And accounts by visitors

15
00:00:32.155 --> 00:00:33.443
like Megasthenes,

16
00:00:33.443 --> 00:00:36.320
Fa-Hien, Hiuen Tsang and Al-Biruni.

17
00:00:36.668 --> 00:00:37.939
Your turn.

18
00:00:37.939 --> 00:00:40.032
Who deciphered Brahmi?
